to look","H4349":"foundation","H2527":"heat","H6703":"dazzling","H216":"light","H5645":"cloud","H2919":"dew","H7105":"harvest","H8552":"to finish","H6525":"flower","H1155":"unripe grape","H1580":"to wean","H1961":"to be","H5328":"flower","H3772":"cut","H2150":"tendril","H4211":"pruner","H853":"[Obj.]","H5189":"tendril","H5493":"remove","H8456":"to strike away","H5800":"forsake","H3162":"unitedness","H5861":"bird of prey","H929":"animal","H6972":"to summer","H2778":"to taunt","H6256":"time","H2986":"to conduct","H7862":"gift","H6635":"army","H4725":"place","H8034":"name","H6726":"Zion"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["challoner",2,null,"challoner","Angels.... Or messengers."],["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Cymbal. Or sistrum, commonly used in Egypt. Septuagint, “ship sails.” — Ethiopia, or Chus, lying between the Nile (the branches of which are styled rivers) and the Red Sea. He alludes to the kingdom of Tharaca, 4 Kings xix. 8. (Calmet)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Ambassadors. Hebrew, “images,” (Bochart) in honour of Adonis; (St. Cyril) or rather Ezechias, or Tharaca send to demand troops. (Calmet) — Bulrushes. Literally, “paper.” (Haydock) — Formed of rushes which grow on the banks of the Nile. (Pliny, [Natural History?] vii. 56., and xiii. 11.) — Angels. Or messengers. — Pieces. With factions after the death of Sabacon, or by the inroads of Sennacherib. — Other. He derides the vanity of the Egyptians. (Calmet) — Expecting the overflowing of the Nile. (Haydock) — Hebrew, “of line,” (Calmet) with which they marked out each person’s property, after the waters had subsided. (Strabo 17.) — Foot. They worked their dough with their feet, and sent swine to trample on the seed, which required no more cultivation. (Herodotus ii. 14., and 36.) — Spoiled. The Nile made considerable alterations."],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Place. God rules all with ease. — Harvest. The allies shall comfort my people, (Calmet) or Sennacherib shall threaten ruin. (Haydock) — But I will frustrate his evil designs. His army shall perish unexpectedly, ver. 5. (Calmet) — The Egyptians had sent messengers to assure the Israelites that they would come to assist them: but the prophet informs them of their own ruin. (Worthington)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Them. Their bodies shall lie unburied."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Sion. Egypt shall send presents to the Lord, 2 Paralipomenon xxxii. 23. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"],"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}